content.text:
Now add movement assertions to yard-purpose test. We need to position early: after `goOutside`, sample Charlie position, wait 1.5s, sample again, assert moved > 12 px. But then our test immediately calls `callGirlsOutside` etc.; we can insert after fresh-air assertions and before call girls. However waiting 1.5s allows mood/history comparisons? The before/after were already captured. Fine. But elapsed changes may not matter. Add:
```js
const c0=await page.evaluate(...);
await page.waitForTimeout(1500);
const c1=...
assert(Math.hypot(c1.x-c0.x,c1.y-c0.y)>15,'Charlie runs around the yard instead of standing still');
assert(!/finding a way around the doorway/.test(c1.agenda),'Charlie uses yard movement language');
```
Need to ensure he's moving along a fence route and perhaps near x>800 to test fence? Movement alone proves. We could also wait until he's along fence. But random route could go across the yard. Keep movement generic. Add a test for fence barking? The existing temporary yard2-test isn't in suite. We should add a proper test in the suite: spawn a car near Charlie and wait? Cars spawn randomly along street x=965 y=-30 down. Charlie near fence barks if within 190. We can force a car by manipulating? Cars array not exposed directly, only state snapshot. We could add debug `cars` reference and push a car. Expose `cars` in debug (it's a `let` binding; `__game.cars` can be a getter? Just add `cars` to object, it's a reference at init; but `cars` is reassigned via `cars=cars.filter(...)`, so a reference would go stale. Expose `spawnCar` debug function or `addCar` that pushes. Simpler: add `spawnCar:()=>cars.push({x:965,y:145,vy:0})` in debug. Then test: position Charlie near fence? He moves; force position x=875,y=145 and clear route; add car at x=965,y=145; wait 100ms; read bubble 'Woof! Car!'. But the yard block will immediately set a route; doesn't matter. However `charlieTimer.barkLeft` maybe 0. The bark check runs each frame before? Order in yard block: first route assignment, then bark check. With near car, bubble sets. Good.

But adding `spawnCar` debug just for test is okay; we already expose lots. Or push via `__game.setCars`? Let's add `spawnCar:(x=965,y=160,vy=0)=>cars.push({x,y,vy})`.

Test:
```js
await page.evaluate(() => {
  const c=__game.girls.find(g=>g.dog); c.x=875;c.y=160;c.route=[];
  __game.spawnCar(965,160,0);
});
await page.waitForTimeout(120);
const bark=await page.evaluate(()=>({bubble:...,agenda:...}));
assert(/Woof! Car!/.test(bark.bubble),'Charlie barks at cars by the fence');
```
But car at (965,160), Charlie at (875,160) distance 90 <190. Yes. But `cars.forEach(c=>c.y+=c.vy*dt)` with vy=0 stays. Great. Car drawing at y=160. Then remove car? Not necessary; it stays until y<H+40; with vy=0, it remains forever! That would cause Charlie to bark continuously (barkLeft cooldown 2.5) but not harmful. However it could interfere with later test assertions? No. But test may end with car. Could set vy=-100 to move up out; or expose no. Better spawn with `vy:-200` so it moves up off-screen and removed. But then within 120ms it moves 24px to y=136, still close. Good. Use vy:-200.

Wait, car filter is `cars.filter(c=>c.y<H+40)`, moving up negative means y<740 always true, never removed! It would go off top and stay. Oops. Spawn y=-30 with vy positive is how they move. If we spawn at y=160 with vy=+200, it moves down and removed at 740 after ~3s. Fine. Use vy=120.

Also, our test's focus on Charlie's movement: after we set c.x=875,y=160, that's fine.

Where to put bark test? After movement assertion. Then wait. Then continue call girls. Charlie's bubble may persist while call tests happen but no assertions on Charlie. Good.

Need to ensure test spawns car after movement; only one car maybe existing. Fine.

Add `spawnCar` to debug.

